Surface intersection: the parts of an open line beyond its vertices
(mitcad#263).

GeomInt_LineConstructor splits an analytic line of two surfaces (a line,
parabola or hyperbola) at its vertices and keeps the parts between them
that lie in both surfaces' domains. A line with no vertex is kept whole;
with two or more, the parts before the first vertex and after the last were
dropped although they lie in both domains (on unbounded surfaces always).
A cone and a plane parallel to its axis meet in a hyperbola whose asymptotes
are the cone's generatrices parallel to the plane: where the cone's seam is
one of them within rounding (a chamfer's cone with its seam 8.6e-9 rad off
the direction of a wall square to it), the hyperbola crosses the seam twice
some 1e8 mm away, and of the branch through the wall only a piece of no
length between those two vertices was left. The chamfer then found no cut
of its cone by the wall and failed (it went on to a walking that chamfers
do not implement).

- GeomInt_LineConstructor::Perform, for such open lines with vertices: the
  part from the line's start to its first vertex and from its last vertex
  to its end are tested at a point inside them (one unit from the vertex on
  an unbounded side) as the parts between vertices are, and kept when in
  both domains; parts that meet are joined, so that a line wholly in both
  domains comes out as one part (the whole line, as with no vertices).

Upstream: new.
